{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/540510-so-many-sidequests","id":540510,"title":"So Many Sidequests","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-06-04T21:11:49-07:00","id":540510,"title":"So Many Sidequests","content":"To be honest, what I'm going to get into here is a 2e or even World of Darkness problem in general. That said, I'm running a Vampire game, and Vampires have a few specific issues beyond what the rest of the nWoD has.<br /> <br /> And the issue I'm going to get into is, for lack of a better term, side-quests.<br /> <br /> That is, there is an awful lot to be done outside of whatever main plot you prepare for your players. There are feeding scenes (recommended at least once every session). There are Aspirations, which you, as Storyteller, are expected to work into the story whatever they are. Related to Aspirations is the political game that vampires are generally expected to be a part of. There's social maneuvering, which takes days or weeks to perform if you aren't just forcing doors every time.<br /> <br /> I run an online game, which admittedly tends to get less done in the same period of time, but we go up to eight hours on the weekend. Even with all that time, I just roll for all the feeding scenes. I don't feel like there's enough time for a dedicated mini-game every session. How do I make time for a project like &quot;research this ancient artifact&quot; without splitting the party and leaving everyone else bored while I handle one person's aspiration? How do you make time for politics? How do you get the players into the politics when they just flounder every time you try? How do you implement social maneuvering, when every other game I'm aware of (and even prior editions of the Storyteller system) encourage completing social interactions with some roleplay and a singular roll of the dice? Players generally seem to want to just get it done and move on to their main goal.<br /> <br /> I feel like my campaigns are just Hunter: the Vampire Edition because I can't figure out how to fit all these various systems and mechanics into a campaign. I end up just going for the same &quot;present problem, hunt down problem, solve (usually murder) problem&quot; setup that I would use in any other system. I'm not really satisfied with this. I want to use Vampire: the Requiem 2e to its full potential.<br /> <br /> Anyone have similar experiences or suggestions?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1692-maina","name":"Maina","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1692_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"724"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-04T21:20:52-07:00","id":540537,"title":"","content":"I know it's easy to get side-tracked, that's sort of a price for these kinds of games.<br /> <br /> But I think part of the trick is having the character aspirations tie into the main plot. After that the politics can be, really, ignored or just happen without the players, providing they're not a part of the main plot or player aspirations. While feeding scenes are pretty important, there are times you can skip ahead and have some happen during the fade to black, 'You spend a couple weeks tracking down rumors of a dealer who accepts trade in blood, and while doing so manage to get some yourself.'<br /> <br /> The length of time involved with the social maneuvering system, among other things, has kept me from using it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-05T03:28:31-07:00","id":540832,"title":"","content":"I think this is a common complaint that many a GM has run into; I know I certainly have from time to time. Perhaps it's a holdover from players having a lot of their RPG experience playing games like Pathfinder and D&amp;D, but the general format for many players is to barrel about your game world trying to murder-solve any problem they're presented with. Which is fine in some occasions but can become counter-productive if you find them wheeling out the same strategy to every problem you throw at them. Perhaps its also a symptom of the way our generation/s are perhaps a little too spoiled with immediate gratification type games and have little time for the hard work of deep role playing unless its shoved down their throats. <br /> <br /> In any case, you might try blocking off the possibility of a simple 'kill the problem' type approach and try layering the solution to the problem in a complex world political power players, many of whom might or might not have the influence, contacts, information, blood sorcery, that your players require in order to cobble together a solution. Prevent them from just killing a party just to obtain the plot 'point' they need to complete their quest. Make sure there are some terrible and potentially far reaching consequences if they should start pulling out guns, knives and claws. Force them to fill out their social rolls with in-character role playing before an NPC responds with anything useful. If they get frustrated, fine, let them get used to living in a world where it takes a little patience and a little hard work to get the rewards.<br /> <br /> Also consider having a look at their character types and aspirations. Ask yourself how someone might go about obtaining something analogous in the real world - what sacrifices or favours would they have to do get enough social credit to enable them to access their desired goals. Its all about the levers of power and who holds the keys to the kingdom. If they want something then they'll have to use their social smarts to get close to those who hold the keys to power. These people should want/need favours, debts, odd jobs, strange occult things, want the players to take part in strange rituals for no apparent reason, etc. But don't above all, let them just fight their way through any problem just get some reward - no matter much they whine out of character. In sum, embed parts of the solution in the social structure of your game and refuse and punish attempts to simply shoot a way to the prizes with nasty social consequences. <br /> <br /> That said, make sure your players can 'see' the way forward in any problem/quest. Often players resort to violence when they cannot see how their characters could progress or what their best course of action should be. You need to make sure that the ways forward are not too opaque and that there is somekind of trail or sign post to point them in the right direction. You don't have to hold their hands of course, but they need to be able to find a path in order to walk it. try to look at the game from a sympathetic point of view rather than as a D&amp;D Gm trying to butcher his players with ever fiendish puzzles and traps (I'm sure you wouldn't anyway)!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-size:14px\"><i>Have you seen him? </i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1000-mistere","name":"MisterE","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1000_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"36"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-05T03:31:43-07:00","id":540834,"title":"","content":"I wouldn't try to use every facet of the game, particularly ones that the players don't show much interest in. Don't include something because you feel required to, use it as a flavour when you have a good idea for it.<br /> <br /> The courtly politics can easily take a back seat, only really featuring when it connects to a specific plot. Likewise social manoeuvring.<br /> <br /> Aspirations and entirely personal plots could be played outside of regular session time, if the players prefer not to be the 'audience' for a while. This also allows the players to have their characters work in secret, if that's their preference - personally I prefer to have the players acting overtly and make sure they can keep OOC and IC knowledge separate.<br /> <br /> A good feeding scene every few sessions is better than a required one every session. Quoting Rose in <a href=\"http://forums.whitewolfarchive.com/defaultbd09.html\" target=\"_blank\">the old forum archive</a>: &quot;If you're playing <i>Vampire</i>, and things start to slow down, <b>ask who's hungry</b>. Almost inevitably, someone will be running low on Vitae. Asking them how they look for food is a good chance to dig into their character's relationship with humanity, and give you a chance to complicate things.&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Craig Oxbrow<br /> <b>The Trinity Continuum</b> freelancer","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/221-craig-oxbrow","name":"Craig Oxbrow","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar221_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"608"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-05T05:08:12-07:00","id":541004,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Maina</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/540510-so-many-sidequests#post540510\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">To be honest, what I'm going to get into here is a 2e or even World of Darkness problem in general. That said, I'm running a Vampire game, and Vampires have a few specific issues beyond what the rest of the nWoD has.<br /> <br /> And the issue I'm going to get into is, for lack of a better term, side-quests.</div> </div> </div> </div> I'm at a loss how this is a specific Vampire problem. Everyone has so-called side quests, in my honest opinion. Werewolves need to shapeshift daily, and then there's hunting. Mages have their pet projects and experiments. Changelings seek out their fetches and try to &quot;return home&quot; so they're only Lost in name, not truth. Promethean is effectively a game built around Golconda seekers, so side quests are the whole point of that game.<br /> <br /> Everyone has Aspirations - if its a problem in Vampire for your group, but not any other game, than its a curious thing, but I wouldn't think that's common. For vampire, aspirations tend to be political-social. In werewolf, they tend to be related to territory building or hunting, or, yes, political-socializing for above territory building. Same in mage, but with their own personal mysteries they're solving to study and grow in magic.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Anyone have similar experiences or suggestions? </div> </div> </div> Nofather actually hit it on the head, in my opinion. Tie everything into the main plot. You'll notice this a lot in movies and novels - if its mentioned at all, you can bet it somehow has relevance to the story. If there's a territory dispute subplot, feeding scenes feed into that - you spot poachers in your territory. Skim over the unnecessary parts, but do include things that are central to the story by weaving it all together.<br /> <br /> Its all about making the player characters care and become invested in. Tie the political climber's needs into the main plot. The Ordo Dracul alchemist needs the heart of a diablarist... and one happens to be part of the main plot. Remember, everything that happens is a potential plot hook. Just make sure the fishing line is generally tied into the main plot's reel.<br /> <br /> (Exception) There are times when the plot needs downtime, so don't tie everything in when its not dramatically appropriate.<br /> <br /> That said....<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I end up just going for the same &quot;present problem, hunt down problem, solve (usually murder) problem&quot; setup that I would use in any other system. </div> </div> </div>Perhaps this is the main problem here. If your games are turning into Vampire: the Vigil, perhaps you should work on some different main plots? This could be why you have such an issue with &quot;side quests.&quot; Your players want to do vampire-y things, but it doesn't resonate with the plot you make? Maybe make the problem into an elder, for whom &quot;murder-hobo&quot; isn't the best answer. <br /> <br /> Take Reap the Whirlwind, for example. The plot STARTS with you killing off the city's Prince. And now, you have to deal with the cluster!@#$ that results from the power vacuum. &quot;Solve the problem via Murder&quot; isn't really a solution here, not if they dont' want an entire city either trying to kill them, or pretty much killing off the entire city themselves. <br /> <br /> Make it so that murder as a solution has consequences. This is the WoD, not D&amp;D. Killing has negative ramifications - attention from police, potential hunters, friends and family seeking revenge (potentially through manipulating covenant status and allies), power vacuums, etc. Make a point of calling for that humanity loss if they're constantly killing, which can be manifested with hunting problems as people avoid the characters.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2102,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","user":"MCN","date":". 06-05-2015, 08:20 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-05T12:48:53-07:00","id":541607,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/540510-so-many-sidequests?p=540537#post540537\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But I think part of the trick is having the character aspirations tie into the main plot.</div> </div> </div> </div>Honestly, I'd really say that the game works best when Aspirations <i>are</i> your main plot. 2nd ed takes a lot from some of the more modern games like Fate, which tend to be much more &quot;Players create characters...Storyteller generates main plot based on the things important to those characters.&quot;<br /> <br /> If you can buy the PDF, it might be worthwhile to take a look at the Adventure-creation section from the Dresden Files RPG (&quot;Your Story&quot;). Aspirations aren't Aspects, but you can use them as the foundation of crafting your stories in a similar way.<br /> <br /> Think of it like Hamlet - Hamlet's &quot;Avenge My Father&quot; Aspiration isn't a side quest to the main plot of Hamlet, it <i>is</i> the main plot of Hamlet!<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2007-wade-l","name":"Wade L","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2007_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"136"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-05T12:58:56-07:00","id":541614,"title":"","content":"Yeah, but people come at games differently.<br /> <br /> With some games a Storyteller has an idea about a certain situation, and wants to run the players through that plot. A problem with this is that they can often overlook the players Aspirations or personal goals. For others, the players come up with characters and they have a plan for that character, an aspiration that is something they really want the game to get into. A problem with this is if it's not fully appreciated by the storyteller, it can take things widely off track. I have a general rule, 'Do whatever you want, but if you're going to fly to Mexico, give me a lot of warning,' because I have not planned anything for Mexico, or Montana or Thailand, but it's entirely possible a character will decide they want to head off somewhere, likely someplace less drastic, based on a single line in their backstory that I overlooked. And sometimes storytellers or players just want to play, and don't have a particular direction where they want things to go, they're content letting the game open up in front of them. In this case you can have games with no-to-little direction, as even Aspirations might not be very helpful if they lean more towards mechanics, like, 'Get the fifth dot of Wits.'<br /> <br /> So it really depends on who's involved, what they want, and a balancing act between it all.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-05T13:51:15-07:00","id":541652,"title":"","content":"Got a quick solution for you. Go on over to drive thru RPG and pick up Silent Legions. It's an old school Lovecraftian Horror game with a totally brilliant system for building mysteries. The short version is that you start with a resolution that ends up something like &quot;A crime is about to be committed by the Enemy against an Actor of Friend in order to advance a Scheme. The clues are pointing towards this grim purpose.&quot; The actors, friends, schemes and enemies are all grabbed from your setting.<br /> <br /> Then you generated a few investigation scenes that will lead to a that resolution, then generate an introduction scene that tells the players what direction they should head in, and finally a hook that gets them invested. Silent Legions also builds in other scenes to pitch the action up or down as needed. The great thing about this is that you can roll up the basic structure in about half an hour, and get a good 6-8 hours of solid gameplay out of it. Of course, you still have to stitch things together and know when to drop scenes or add them. But it gives a good direction without expecting the players to be 100% successful. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"http://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/145769/Silent-Legions\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/images/3482/145769.jpg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/145769/Silent-Legions\" target=\"_blank\"> Silent Legions - Sine Nomine Publishing | DriveThruRPG.com </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/145769/Silent-Legions\" target=\"_blank\"> http://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/145769/Silent-Legions </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> Silent Legions - The world is broken in hidden ways. The truth is concealed for the sake of mortal minds. The unendurable reality that bo </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> I've been using this as my Primary plots, while letting the characters interact with all of their character connections and Aspirations as supporting story. Because the mysteries are always involving people they know or time limits, it tends to focus everyone's attention.<br /> <br /> I can't emphasize enough the going through the NPC mapping that is in the 2e V:TR book. Once you have this map, you can make characters into antagonists that might be friends or benefactors to other characters. For example, here's my group's character map:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B0QGoyHBMghLRXpYYzU4OVExaFU/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B0QGoyHBMghLRXpYYzU4OVExaFU/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\"> </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B0QGoyHBMghLRXpYYzU4OVExaFU/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\"> https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B0QGoyHBMghLRXpYYzU4OVExaFU/view?usp=sharing </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5995-jonwake","name":"JonWake","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5995_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-05T13:57:41-07:00","id":541661,"title":"","content":"What program did you use to generate that, Jon? Looks great!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-05T13:57:58-07:00","id":541662,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JonWake</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/540510-so-many-sidequests?p=541652#post541652\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B0QGoyHBMghLRXpYYzU4OVExaFU/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B0Q...ew?usp=sharing</a></div> </div> </div> </div>What wizardry is this?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-05T14:07:17-07:00","id":541672,"title":"","content":"I went on a lengthy search for a might program to let me do flowcharts. After many a dragon were slain, I found an app for Google Drive called draw.io Pro. It's free, and integrates directly with Google Drive. Its the bee's knees.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5995-jonwake","name":"JonWake","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5995_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-05T14:07:29-07:00","id":541673,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/540510-so-many-sidequests?p=541662#post541662\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> What wizardry is this?</div> </div> </div> </div>It's Climbing the Ladder, my group is also in the process of doing it, great way to generate characters and hooks.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JonWake</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/540510-so-many-sidequests?p=541672#post541672\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I went on a lengthy search for a might program to let me do flowcharts. After many a dragon were slain, I found an app for Google Drive called draw.io Pro. It's free, and integrates directly with Google Drive. Its the bee's knees.</div> </div> </div> </div>Wow, just got this, and it's marvelous, totally makes it possible for our online group to do this. Awesome resource, thanks so much for sharing!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":159,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","user":"Leetsepeak","date":". 06-05-2015, 05:11 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-05T14:16:52-07:00","id":541682,"title":"","content":"I meant the program, but thanks both.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-05T15:03:42-07:00","id":541720,"title":"","content":"Climbing The Ladder is absolutely hilarious fun, and a wonderful tool.<br /> Here is a copy of my groups latest Climbing The Ladder for anyone who is interested in such things!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><i><span style=\"font-size:15px\"><u>Mortals</u></span></i></b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Afrique Hawkins</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> is a tough homicide detective that knows too much about the supernatural, but is not sure what she can do about it. She arrested Lukas once but honestly believes his story about monsters in the night, and she wants to see justice done.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Caitlyn</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> lost her best friend Andrea from high school, and has no idea why they stopped keeping in touch. One day after more than a year, Andrea came to visit looking bloody and wounded... and made false promises that her bite might turn her into a werewolf. Andrea just needed the flesh to heal, and now Caitlyn bears the scars. She is currently a young teacher at the school where Toby attends.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Kat Dennings</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> was an actress that Lisa admired enough that she could not resist arranging a meeting thanks to supernatural persuasion. She regrets falling into frenzy and draining her to death in her trailer. She was childhood friends with both Afrique Hawkins and Lukas Volkmir before she became famous.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Lukas Volkmir</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> was shown mercy when Asher came to collect on some loans, because he had a family. Since that day he has become obsessed with learning about things that go bump during the full moon. He was arrested by Afrique once for stalking Asher later on, and to his surprise she believed his wild stories. He owes Brandon BIG TIME for some reason but does not know the truth about him.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Melody</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> is a loving mother who lost her daughter a while back, and it drove a wedge between herself and her husband. She discovered that Andrea is a werewolf after an accident involving silver Jewelry and suspects that Asher is also a werewolf because they always hang out together.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Samuel Beckerson</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> was a client of Andrea whom she murdered and ate, only to discover that he was not even close to the bad guy she thought he was. He was good friends of Melody and a business partner of Lawrence.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Toby</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> is the little brother of Asher, who always wanted to be just like his big brother until Asher had to put distance between him and his new violent life. He has a crush on Caitlyn, and when she came to work covered in scars he wanted to avenge her.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><i><span style=\"font-size:15px\"><u>Supernaturals</u></span></i></b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Alice</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> is a wise Bone Shadow that seeks to better werewolves from their savage nature and encourage them not to eat humans. She pities Asher for sliding back into his violent habits.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Brandon</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> is the father of Andrea, and a pack leader of the Ivory Claws. He fooled his daughter by hiding her from the Ivory Claw tribe and from the werewolf double life that he leads. With his daughter's safety on the line, he is calling in a favor that Lukas owes him.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Carlos</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> is the apex predator that feeds on other predators. He's the fucking shark that makes all the other sharks decide to fuck off to safer waters to feed. What he loves to do, what his modus operandi is? Force a Werewolf into Wasu-Im again and again and carve off chunks of flesh from the writhing Gauru until he's collected or devoured his fill ... and then lets the wolf go to heal up. Carlos wants something from Brandon in return for not hunting his daughter. He owes Lea Park BIG TIME for some reason.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Fire-In-Her-Eyes</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> was a Fire Touched who Asher used to know from his childhood. Their rivalry grew to violent levels until he finally murdered her upon the field of battle. She has a professional relationship with Brandon, and was pretty good friends with Toby when they were younger.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Lawrence</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> is a flamboyant bastard who seems to know everything and has an unhealthy appreciation for young boys. He is calling in a favor that Radic Kashyk owes him. He was doing business with Samuel Beckerson before Samuel was murdered by Asher.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Lea Park</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> is a badass gangbanger in the same gang as Asher, and she got there by being more cunning and fierce than any other guy. She secretly idolizes Brandon even though he is the enemy, because he inspires loyalty so easily. She is calling in a favor that Carlos owes her.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Radic Kashyk</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> is a russian mob boss who runs the operation where both Asher and Lea Park work. He owes Lawrence BIG TIME for some reason.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Ragnar</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> is an old and powerful Blood Talon that is a little too ambitious for his own good. But he is absolutely savage, and impossibly strong. He idolizes Alice for living up to standards he thinks are otherwise impossible.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">The Palid One</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> had a pack once but something happened after they cleared one of the biggest, snarliest, most fucked up nests of Host in the city - nobody agrees if it was Azlu, Beshilu, or something else, but afterwards... The Palid One is said to live down on the abandoned part of the docks where nobody goes except for hobos and the horribly clueless. The Palid One wants something from Lea Park and Melody, but its mind is unknowable.<br /> <br /> <u><i><b>The Characters</b></i></u></span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Andrea</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> ABANDONED Caitlyn (Best Friend) because of her first change.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Andrea</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> was FOOLED by Brandon (Father) because he hid everything related to werewolves her entire life.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Andrea</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> RESPECTS Alice because of her dedication to the Bone Shadow creed.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Andrea</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> IDOLIZES Alice (Bone Shadow) because she is the model and icon of what a werewolf should be.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Andrea</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> ESCAPED Carlos... after he had already taken a bite out of her.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Andrea</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> FUCKED UP when she put on silver jewelry and started freaking out right in front of Melody's eyes?</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Andrea</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> OWES BIG to Brandon for all his years hiding her from the horrors of the Ivory Claws.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Andrea</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> MURDERED Samuel Beckerson because she thought he was an asshole, but he turned out to be a pretty good guy.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Asher </span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">ABANDONED Toby (Little Brother) because they were too old and life moves on. Asher doesn't want his brother to follow in his footsteps.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Asher</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> was FOOLED by Alice (Bone Shadow), who convinced him that he could live in this violent world without hurting innocent people.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Asher</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> pities and feels bad for Brandon, because he was abandoned by his daughter the way Asher abandoned Toby.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Asher</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> IDOLIZES Lea Park (Gang Member) because she is the model and icon of what a werewolf should be.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Asher</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> thinks The Palid One is INCOMPREHENSIBLE because he was hunting and wounded by the beast, but released afterwards.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Asher</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> REVEALED himself to Lisa while investigating the same situation, but she managed to slip away somehow.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Asher</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> SUSPECTS that Lawrence is a vampire, since he has some of the same habits as Lisa.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Asher</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> OWES BIG Lea Park because she pulled his ass out of the fire and those kind of favors are not easily forgotten.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Asher</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> MURDERED Fire-In-Her-Eyes because he got carried away during a battle and went too damn far.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Lisa</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> ABANDONED Melody (Mother) because she was transformed into a vampire against her will.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Lisa</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> was FOOLED by Lawrence (Lawrence) because she thought he could offer her the answers her sire could not.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Lisa</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> RESPECTS Ragnar (Blood Talon) because he is the savage perfection of what a werewolf should be.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Lisa</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> is TERRIFIED of The Palid One because of what he represents and what she suspects about him.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Lisa</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> REVEALED herself to Asher when she needed to escape from his lupine wrath during an investigation.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Lisa</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> SUSPECTS that Andrea is also a werewolf, using a combination of her own supernatural talents and detective skill.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Lisa</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> OWES BIG to Lawrence because he provided her with favors and information for a long time.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Lisa</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> MURDERED Kat Dennings because she admired her and had a crush on her, but couldn't resist her frenzy once they were alone.</span></span></span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/484-iry","name":"Iry","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar484_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"346"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-05T16:27:49-07:00","id":541801,"title":"","content":"Some thoughts:<br /> <br /> Tying Aspirations into your main plot is a good idea. You might also consider reducing the number of Aspirations characters have if you think your games run slower than average. You can also do Aspirations during downtime, as has been mentioned.<br /> <br /> Changing the time scales for social maneuvering might also help you out. The defaults are meant to cover stories that unfold in steady installments over a fair length of time. I often do night-by-night stories that then have gaps of a few weeks or months in between them, and for something like that, you might want to change the scales.<br /> <br /> Social maneuvering is also more for downtime and long-term projects than it is for stuff like sweet-talking the detective into letting you into the evidence room, which you'd still want a single roll (and maybe some Majesty) for.<br /> <br /> Politics are a fun part of <i>Vampire</i>, but I don't think it's worth forcing it if they don't fit into your storylines. It sounds like you tend towards more procedural stories, so you could use politics more the way department politics play into some cop shows -- as a recurring but non-central complication in the story.<br /> <br /> One feeding scene per session is approximate, and if your sessions are slowing down you might want to do them less often. As Craig quoted above, I find feeding useful to jumpstart action, and if your problem is that you've got too much action for your time frame, then doing it less frequently is totally fine. I don't recommend glossing feeding entirely, because it's where important parts of the horror and drama live.<br /> <br /> As for players getting bored while other people's characters do things, you might consider handing off NPCs to players whose characters aren't in the scene. Whether you can do that with important NPCs varies with how much you're willing to let a player in on the plots, but it can work nicely. In one of my games, there was a recurring character that I ended up always handling to another player, because he actually played that character better than me.<br /> <br /> For those of you who like Climbing the Ladder: the nWoD 2e corebook will feature a similar generator for missing persons story arcs. Think &quot;who killed Laura Palmer?&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Cavaliers of Mars Creator</b><br /> <b>Retired CofD Lead</b><br /> <br /> Check out my <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Rose%20Bailey\" target=\"_blank\">guides to </a><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/261142/BITE-ME-How-to-Write-Vampire?affiliate_id=453494&amp;src=OPPf\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire</a> and my <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/browse/pub/4895/Fantasy-Heartbreaker?affiliate_id=453494&amp;src=OPPf\" target=\"_blank\">indie games</a>!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2-rose-bailey","name":"Rose Bailey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2361"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-06T04:57:55-07:00","id":542449,"title":"","content":"In my own experience, Vampire (any edition) is best treated as a sandbox game. As a storyteller I lay out plot hooks and NPCs. Then I take a look at the characters and their merits/flaws/backgrounds and see what ideas I can come up with. Also of course I listen to the players. Then I literally &quot;play to find out&quot; (to borrow from Apocalypse World). I don't use set plots. I see where the actions of the players take the game. Sometimes I want to experiment with certain scenes, sometimes they dig into a plot or follow their own agendas, sometimes we get sidetracked with little details of their every night life, sometimes I facilitate their aspirations. In the end, player participation and investment turn all the bits and pieces into a satisfying chronicle.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1681-murder-of-crows","name":"Murder-of-Crows","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1681_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"630"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-06T08:37:56-07:00","id":542642,"title":"","content":"Wow. Lots of replies! Thank you, everyone. This will probably be a lengthy reply in its own right.<br /> <br /> @nofather: This is something I try to do. Fit aspirations into the plot, that is. I think part of the problem is that my players don't really grok the concept well. They freeze up and can't think of any, so I let them pick really easy ones like &quot;sing&quot; or &quot;feed&quot; that can really be done without any additional effort on anyone's part. Maybe I should be more strict. I'm kind of a wuss who doesn't want to run my players off by being hard on them.<br /> <br /> @MisterE: I've had my share of players with a DnD/Pathfinder background. They have tended to be the most murderous. The odd thing, though, is my current party (due to people joining and leaving and rejoining and so on) is a pair of social-focused Daeva without so much as two vigor dots between them. They still only ever see combat as the resolution to their problems... they just attempt to ditch the quest entirely if it looks like a fight is going to happen, rather than try to find a way around it. It's an issue I haven't had to deal with before. I usually have one stabby character around if the group wants to take that route. In that sense, I suppose combat already is a non-option. They just seem inclined to run away and ditch the story over working around it. Maybe I need a way to make alternatives seem more obvious. I did basically have a Mekhet ghoul they'd been helping out come up and give away a Bane of a pair of Draugr they had to kill, but they just shoved it off onto someone else anyway. Nearly eight years of doing this, and I still feel like an incompetent Storyteller.<br /> <br /> @Craig Oxbrow: I suppose I feel like I'm using nothing unique to Vampire. It feels like I could run the campaign in just about any other system, and it wouldn't affect the plots. I feel that's doing a disservice to those playing. That said, with some advice from this thread, I'm hoping I can do better.<br /> <br /> @MCN: I'm not sure where it's unclear? I said most of my issues are generic WoD, with some additional ones specific to Vampire. Like feeding scenes or the implied focus on politics which is greater than I've read in the other games. Each line has their own particular quirks and concerns. Everyone has side-quests, as you pointed out, but they are not the same for every line, which you also pointed out. I feel you answered your own question, really. That said, I do like the idea of making it so combat isn't an option. I don't generally feel clever enough for it, though, and my current party (a fairly recent development) avoids combat like the plague anyway. I suppose I just need clearer alternate paths so that they take one instead of just abandoning the plot.<br /> <br /> @Wade L: Even before I started playing 2e, I've always wanted to write my plots based around the goals of the players. The problem is I've never had players willing to write up goals. 2e hasn't really changed that with most aspirations being things like the aforementioned &quot;sing&quot; and &quot;feed.&quot; It's frustrating, but I don't know how to help them be more motivated. Well, I've had motivated players before, but even they wouldn't write down goals. I don't know if it's an issue on my end or on theirs.<br /> <br /> @JonWake: I have so many GM aides to try and cover for how terrible I am. I am always welcoming more of them. Thanks for the tip. I'll pick it up when I can afford it.<br /> <br /> @Rose Bailey: Thank you for your response. I hold your opinion in fairly high esteem as one of the few devs whose work I find consistently awesome. Reducing the number of aspirations worries me a bit. Given how hard a time my players have with them as it is, they do not typically get many beats per session from Aspirations as it is. But I suppose two Aspirations they can achieve is better than three they flounder with. I'll give it more thought. The idea of using politics as background rather than focus is one I've been getting a lot since I posted this, off-forums as well. I'll probably try to start doing that, rather than force it where neither I nor the players know how to deal with it. I did try offering to let the PCs play NPCs for a scene the last time it popped up. It was a villain that player had written for the Ladder that another PC was trying to wheedle information out of. They declined, but maybe if it's normalized via offering more regularly, they'll warm up to the idea.<br /> <br /> @Murder-of-Crows: I'm going to be honest here. Since my very first days of GMing (first with the intro scenario to Exalted, then a full campaign of Changeling: the Lost that eventually became crossover), a sandbox game has kind of been my platonic ideal of a tabletop RPG campaign. In my mind, the perfect campaign is a sandbox with things going on independent of the players that they can involve themselves with. The thought of running such a game still fills me with glee. That said, I've never been able to manage it. I've tried, a few times, but each time the players sat around doing absolutely nothing unless I gave them a standard plot hook. Either I'm incapable of running this kind of game (and would like to learn if people have tips), or I've just never had players that play well with the idea (a harder problem to solve).<br /> <br /> Everyone talking about Climbing the Ladder: Good gods. I've gone through this twice. Once with a group of two, once with a group of three. Both times it took two sessions plus some wrap-up on a third, totaling over 12 hours of time each. The end result both times was a list of NPCs that the players never brought up again and who they more or less treated as totally new people if I brought them into the game. There was maybe one NPC each time that actually had any kind of impact on the PC's roleplay. I love the concept, but honestly it felt like a gigantic waste of time both times. Once again, I'm willing to admit I was maybe just doing it wrong somehow.<br /> <br /> In closing: Good golly, I probably over-replied. I didn't want anyone to feel ignored, since everyone had excellent advice and suggestions. Thank you all. I'm open to anything more people have to say, too.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1692-maina","name":"Maina","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1692_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"724"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-06T10:21:24-07:00","id":542733,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Iry</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/540510-so-many-sidequests?p=541720#post541720\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Lisa</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> MURDERED Kat Dennings because she admired her and had a crush on her, but couldn't resist her frenzy once they were alone.</span></span></span></div> </div> </div> </div>Is that -the- <a href=\"http://www.imdb.com/name/nm0993507/\" target=\"_blank\">Kat Dennings</a>? Just asking because I made a thread about people using 'real' people in their games, and I've always been interested in the idea of them attempting to maintain their job in face of the changes to their lifestyle due to whatever they've become. <a href=\"http://www.imdb.com/name/nm1555340/?ref_=fn_al_nm_1\" target=\"_blank\">Alison Brie</a> became a vampire in our vampire game, which was interesting.<br /> <br /> @Maina That is a prohibitively long time. I was going to try a variant for an experiment with Werewolf, I'll see how long it takes for us.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-06T17:48:16-07:00","id":543269,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Maina</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/540510-so-many-sidequests?p=542642#post542642\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">@Rose Bailey: Thank you for your response. I hold your opinion in fairly high esteem as one of the few devs whose work I find consistently awesome. Reducing the number of aspirations worries me a bit. Given how hard a time my players have with them as it is, they do not typically get many beats per session from Aspirations as it is. But I suppose two Aspirations they can achieve is better than three they flounder with. I'll give it more thought. The idea of using politics as background rather than focus is one I've been getting a lot since I posted this, off-forums as well. I'll probably try to start doing that, rather than force it where neither I nor the players know how to deal with it. I did try offering to let the PCs play NPCs for a scene the last time it popped up. It was a villain that player had written for the Ladder that another PC was trying to wheedle information out of. They declined, but maybe if it's normalized via offering more regularly, they'll warm up to the idea.</div> </div> </div> </div>I also somewhat struggled with short term aspirations. The solution at my table was to make every aspiration a long term aspiration, then reward a beat during any game where meaningful time was spent towards that aspiration.<br /> <br /> For example, Jane Doe might have the aspiration to investigate the death of her father. This is not something to be resolved within a single game, but any game where Jane Doe spends a scene investigating or discovers an important clue... she gets a beat at the end of the game. It's an entire phase of our wrap -- &quot;Alright, who made progress on their aspirations tonight? Jane? Yeah, you definitely did. Take a beat!&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Maina</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/540510-so-many-sidequests?p=542642#post542642\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Everyone talking about Climbing the Ladder: Good gods. I've gone through this twice. Once with a group of two, once with a group of three. Both times it took two sessions plus some wrap-up on a third, totaling over 12 hours of time each. The end result both times was a list of NPCs that the players never brought up again and who they more or less treated as totally new people if I brought them into the game. There was maybe one NPC each time that actually had any kind of impact on the PC's roleplay. I love the concept, but honestly it felt like a gigantic waste of time both times. Once again, I'm willing to admit I was maybe just doing it wrong somehow.</div> </div> </div> </div>It definitely takes a chunk of time. 4 hours once (4 players), 6 hours once (5 players), 4 hours again (3 players), 5 hours again (4 players). But we award experience for doing Climbing The Ladder as if it were a real game session -- it practically is!<br /> <br /> As for your players never bringing them up again, and not remembering them when YOU bring them up... That is an entirely different issue.<br /> What's the story with your group? Why are they forgetting so easily?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/540510-so-many-sidequests?p=542733#post542733\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Is that -the- <a href=\"http://www.imdb.com/name/nm0993507/\" target=\"_blank\">Kat Dennings</a>? Just asking because I made a thread about people using 'real' people in their games, and I've always been interested in the idea of them attempting to maintain their job in face of the changes to their lifestyle due to whatever they've become. <a href=\"http://www.imdb.com/name/nm1555340/?ref_=fn_al_nm_1\" target=\"_blank\">Alison Brie</a> became a vampire in our vampire game, which was interesting.</div> </div> </div> </div>It definitely is. This kind of thing normally does not happen in my games, but it is the modern world with most of the same people. The vampire character wanted to make it happen, used majesty to meet her, and couldn't resist a hunger frenzy during the meeting.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":484,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/484-iry","user":"Iry","date":". 06-06-2015, 09:13 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/484-iry","name":"Iry","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar484_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"346"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-06T18:29:41-07:00","id":543294,"title":"","content":"I think its time to sit down with your group and ask them what they want out of the game. Than tell them what you want so you guys can come to a mutual understanding it's what my group does whenever the ST and PC's get unsync'd. Also for the two daeva that run away from combat maybe you should start introducing or using the social combat (doors and whatnot more). You can't tell a good story with out conflict and combat has always been a good way to set pace of a story.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5950-mal","name":"Mal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5950_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"25"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-06T18:33:12-07:00","id":543297,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Iry</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/540510-so-many-sidequests?p=543269#post543269\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It definitely is. This kind of thing normally does not happen in my games, but it is the modern world with most of the same people. The vampire character wanted to make it happen, used majesty to meet her, and couldn't resist a hunger frenzy during the meeting.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hah, cool, I wanted to do it too. We also had Oda Nobunaga but Alison is more comparable to Kat.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}